"Structures a decision hierarchy and derives relative priorities from explicit pairwise comparisons of its elements.\n\nUse when: Elicit tradeoffs when direct criterion weights are difficult. Review the internal consistency of judgments across several decision levels.\n\nAvoid when: Criteria have strong feedback dependencies that a simple hierarchy cannot represent.\n\nVisual grammar: A hierarchy separates the goal, criteria, optional subcriteria and alternatives. Pairwise matrices compare peer elements against their parent. Priority annotations summarize derived weights rather than arbitrary ranks.\n\nBuild: Define a goal and a nonredundant hierarchy of decision criteria. Collect reciprocal pairwise judgments using a declared scale. Derive priorities, inspect inconsistency and aggregate alternative preferences.\n\nCheck: Pairwise comparisons refer to the same parent criterion. Reciprocal judgments and diagonal values are valid. Consistency and sensitivity are reviewed before treating priorities as decisive.\n\nPitfalls: Adding overlapping criteria that count an advantage twice. Reporting a ranked result without the judgments that produced it.\n\nExample: A nonprofit prioritizes candidate sites by access, affordability and capacity, using pairwise judgments and a documented consistency review.\n\nAlternatives: Weighted decision matrix, Decision tree."
